WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.853 --> 00:00:03.030
- [Announcer] A Corona
virus vaccine is seen

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.400
as a key weapon against the pandemic,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.533
but how much will shots cost in the US?

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.710
Some companies have said
that a dose could sell

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.960
for as little as a few
bucks to as much as $25.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.860
And that vaccines may actually
be available fairly quickly

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.830
by industry standards,
meaning less than two years,

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.670
as opposed to more than 10.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.840
- It's called operation warp speed.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.860
That means big. And it means fast.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.000
- [Announcer] Of more
than 160 groups racing

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.170
to put a vaccine on the market,

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.110
a handful entered the final testing stage

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.430
within the first six months.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.490
That's much faster than the usual process,

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.100
which can take years.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.040
And that's in part thanks
to US government money.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.820
The administration handed
out more than $10 billion

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.180
between March and August.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.940
- There's an expectation on the
public's part that the drug,

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.840
because it's been funded
partially by tax payer money,

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.023
that the price will be reasonable.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.740
- [Announcer] But investors
have also poured funds

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.140
into drug makers and stocks

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.760
at some pharma companies have sought.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.540
That's putting pressure on big pharma

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:06.890
to make vaccines affordable

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.993
while also keeping investors happy.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.320
We wanted to know what does that mean

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.683
for the final price tag?

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.300
Developing a vaccine is a huge gamble.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.740
Getting it from the lab to
clinic can cost billions

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.030
of dollars and not all
of the companies that

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:26.920
are developing vaccines will
end up with a viable product.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.220
- Of all of the vaccines
that start human testing,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.630
about 66% of them will fail.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:34.420
- Kenneth Kaitin studies drug pricing

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.210
at Tufts Center for the
Study of Drug Development,

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.100
which receives grants from
the pharmaceutical industry.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.337
He says vaccine R&amp;D is something
few companies can afford.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:47.017
- Not a lot of companies
actually invest in this

00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:48.360
for a variety of reasons.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.500
One of them is the poor
likelihood of success

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.620
and the difficulty of generating revenue

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.340
on whatever R&amp;D activity they have.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.040
- [Announcer] But in the case
of the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.020
government money is taking
away a lot of that risk

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.380
by funding, vaccine
research and development

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.930
and signing contracts to
buy the vaccines before

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.123
they're even approved.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.010
Two government deals with
Moderna and Pfizer would secure

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.710
as many as 200 million
doses by January next year.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.070
And that would mean both
vaccines would be free of charge

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.950
to people in the US at least initially.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.630
To speed things up, pharma companies

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.150
received additional
funds for manufacturing

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.490
and are already mass producing vaccines

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:32.160
while still conducting clinical trials.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.210
Michael Kinch is a biochemistry professor

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.170
and a vaccine historian.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.230
He says producing and storing vaccines

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:39.680
can be very expensive.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.940
- Under what conditions, what
the temperatures and humidity

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.320
and a number of things that, again,

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.110
oftentimes can take years to develop.

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.650
It might be that we find out

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.923
that certain conditions
ruin a batch of vaccine.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.250
- There's also a parallel
scramble to secure billions

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.377
of medical grade vials
and syringes that are just

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.773
as important as clinical trials.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.900
And the US isn't the only country trying

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.622
to secure doses for its citizens.

00:03:05.622 --> 00:03:07.520
- Certainly there are
just 7 billion people,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.468
which is probably an underestimate,

00:03:09.468 --> 00:03:11.970
and if each one perhaps needs two doses,

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.990
you're talking about a
monumental logistics challenge

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:20.400
of being able to create
double digit billions

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.810
of different vaccine doses.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.190
- Manufacturers of glass
vials have estimated

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.710
that worldwide demand
because of the pandemic

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.773
will increase by 2 billion
over the next two years.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.860
Since most of the medical
supplies are sourced overseas,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.480
the US government is giving
American glass-makers

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.080
and syringe suppliers, federal contracts

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.483
to ramp up production.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.170
Some Americans are hesitant
about taking a COVID-19 vaccine

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.520
because of concerns that
development is being rushed.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.940
To allay those concerns,
several industry rivals

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.240
are planning a joint pledge to
not seek regulatory approval

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.133
until the shots are proven
to be safe and effective.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.870
Despite the hurdles, some
companies are joining

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.680
the crowded race because they already have

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.773
the technical capabilities to do so.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.130
For instance, Novavax says
it makes business sense

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.230
to repurpose the technology it uses for

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:17.943
a promising flu vaccine to
develop one for COVID-19.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.030
Plus financial backing from
not just the US government,

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.690
but also various countries and nonprofits

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.330
have also given companies
more of an incentive to chip

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.250
in than ever before.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.550
In return, companies may get
positive coverage worldwide

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.810
for stepping in during a pandemic

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.210
or being one of those to
find an effective vaccine.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.980
Lawmakers have said that drug makers

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.790
should be held accountable
to taxpayer dollars.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.460
Some companies have said their vaccines

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.490
will be priced at cost
because of the current crisis.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.970
- We will be providing a vaccine

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.770
at a not-for-profit price
during the emergency pandemic.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.670
- But Pfizer and Moderna
has said they still intend

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.150
to make a profit.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.960
- We will not sell it cost.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.140
- Kenneth Kayton from Tufts points out

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.560
that some pharmaceutical
companies with portfolios

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.160
of blockbuster drugs
should be able to absorb

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.533
the cost of the vaccine.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.460
Sales from these products
can help take away some of

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:15.513
the pressure to profit from a
single Corona virus vaccine.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.870
Meanwhile, the government is working

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.610
with commercial insurers as
well as Medicare and Medicaid

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.713
so people won't have to pay out of pocket.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.830
There's also a government
plan to help fund

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.210
the vaccines for the uninsured.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.095
Black and Latino
communities have less access

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:32.043
to health care than white people.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.410
And they've been dying at
disproportionately higher rates

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.420
during the pandemic while
also making up a large part

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.910
of essential workers at
greater risk for exposure

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:41.853
to the virus.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.240
So a free or affordable
vaccine would be one

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.410
of the best ways to
protect the most vulnerable

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.613
and return to some level of normalcy.

00:05:52.798 --> 00:05:55.381
(gentle music)

